  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe occluded low pressure system now centered over northern \nWisconsin will continue to progress east-northeast into Lake \nSuperior and southern Ontario this evening and overnight. This will \nallow a cold front to traverse across SE MI this evening, with a \nbroken line of showers likely developing along the boundary. A dry \nslot will build in in the wake of the front as shallow boundary \nlayer mixing commences due to cold air advection. This will bring a \nperiod of dry weather overnight with temperatures falling into the \nupper 40s. Wrap around moisture from the departing low in \nconjunction with subtle moisture advection from Lake Michigan under \nwest low-level flow with the broader upper-level trough will renew \nrain chances later in the day on Thursday and through Friday morning.\nA passage of a secondary cold front will also provide additional \nforcing and will bring in even cooler air with 850 temperatures \ndropping down into the negative single digits by Friday morning. \n\nA strong upper-level wave will drive south into the Great Lakes \nFriday morning before closing off as it progresses into Lake Erie by \nSaturday morning. This feature will strengthen cyclonic low to mid-\nlevel flow, bringing rain chances through Friday especially across \nthe Tri-Cities and Thumb where better moisture advection coming off \nof Lake Huron resides. Seasonal normal values settle in Friday and \nSaturday. Highs in the 50s and lows in the 30s are expected. As the \nupper-level wave departs, influence of the thermal trough will \nweaken, allowing for a subtle rise in highs through the mid-week \nperiod albeit still more in line with seasonal normals. Another \ntrough will build in across Lake Huron Sunday into Monday providing \nanother chance for rain showers, but otherwise there will be a pretty\nmuted signal for precipitation chances into the midweek period.",
